#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167580} :activity/id #uuid "494e7d10-63cc-5ac1-aeb6-77e53987c48f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048333 :topic/id #uuid "95fa9a7c-5a38-5e0a-9686-d1451bc45075" :topic/math-academy-id 3212} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167580 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168064 :tutorial/content "We've already seen how to write the coordinates of a vector $\\mathbf v\\in \\mathbb R^n$ relative some general basis $\\mathcal B$ of $\\mathbb R^n.$ In this lesson, we will generalize this concept to abstract vector spaces.\n\nAs an example, consider the matrices $A_1$ and $A_2,$ given by\n\n![](images/28/2828c037a750f078b4d98b93248e800ec05d4899d7eb390dd135326314c44e70.svg)\n\nThe set $\\mathcal{B}=\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}$ is a basis of the vector space $V = \\textrm{Span}\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}.$ Now, suppose that the coordinates of the matrix $C$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ are \n\n![](images/2d/2db3ab1e4452eff2f19151cc87a805452433c7f305a80e5480c25241cba42c38.svg)\n\nLet's find the matrix $C$ using this information. \n\nAs usual, any vector in $\\textrm{Span}\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}$ can be written as\n\n$$\nx_1 A_1  + x_2 A_2,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1,x_2$ are the coordinates of the vector relative to the basis. \n\nIn our case, we have \n\n![](images/64/6489e130a9237881664e1649cc5408cdff87cfb12dcf44c0342b9961cdf7bc81.svg)\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]+4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 12 \\\\ 8 & -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 10 \\\\ 8 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6638787f-3ef3-5623-bcec-de4b89ebfd02" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4962 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e61da532-1350-59ec-be76-0a0464423ad6" :step/math-academy-id 653397 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167581}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166751 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166589 :question/id #uuid "4dac81da-f15c-55ff-9630-7349a3e03e57" :question/math-academy-id "e-8273"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf357ee2-b8b4-538c-b269-f7546cf5374f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048552}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559880}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector Given Its Coordinates Relative to a Basis"} :step/id #uuid "9a4ae83d-c5c3-5b9f-bd08-97b2e0bc398a" :step/math-academy-id 653398 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167582}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167582 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166752 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166590 :question/id #uuid "aac4b559-b26b-57b0-8701-b3235969cd2f" :question/math-academy-id "e-8274"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0a6b6e1-fb99-5ebe-9f3f-000878f79f0d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049058}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565478}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector With Respect to a Given Basis in a Matrix Vector Space"} :step/id #uuid "8e896c1e-d15c-5ab8-ab0f-39877293d9e4" :step/math-academy-id 653401 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167583}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167583 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166753 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166591 :question/id #uuid "f0fc412f-8276-5876-a509-4f2173396e72" :question/math-academy-id "e-8275"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d8f8be0-0fe9-5734-87d3-5c432b7f298f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565445}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector With Respect to a Given Basis in a Polynomial Vector Space"} :step/id #uuid "6b14a3b6-adad-5d10-bce3-5e05e7cf5144" :step/math-academy-id 653404 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167584}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167584 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168065 :tutorial/content "Suppose we fix a basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{b}_n \\}$ of a finite-dimensional vector space $V$ with scalars from $\\mathbb{R}$ (note that \"finite-dimensional\" means that the basis contains a finite number of elements). Then, we can represent any element $\\mathbf{v} \\in V$ using the coordinates of $\\mathbf{v}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/c9/c9297a30f87baf08295a752cb23f2d314139508f875b4ffa2fffc8763656bd54.svg)\n\nThis defines a bijective mapping $\\phi: V\\to \\mathbb{R}^n.$ Moreover, this particular mapping has some nice properties:\n\n- $\\phi(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}) = \\phi(\\mathbf{u}) + \\phi(\\mathbf{v})$ for any $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in V,$ and\n\n- $\\phi(\\alpha \\mathbf{v}) = \\alpha \\phi(\\mathbf{v})$ for any $\\mathbf{v} \\in V$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nBecause of this, we say that $V$ is **isomorphic** to $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ Intuitively, this means that $V$ and $\\mathbb R^n$ have identical structures.\n\nSo, any finite-dimensional vector space is isomorphic to $\\mathbb{R}^n$ for some particular $n.$ This means that instead of studying all possible vector spaces, we can simply study only one family of vector spaces, namely $\\mathbb{R}^n$ for $n\\geq 1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e501b24f-7ea0-5110-a4c5-e8739ea9ac11" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4964 :tutorial/title "Isomorphisms of Vector Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "f13dd7dd-f256-5871-9c5b-37fcd35b85a3" :step/math-academy-id 653407}] :activity/title "The Coordinates of a Vector Relative to a Basis in Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166996}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497483} :activity/id #uuid "7a5a6986-cd36-531a-9f9c-dcfb6003f956" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048333 :topic/id #uuid "95fa9a7c-5a38-5e0a-9686-d1451bc45075" :topic/math-academy-id 3212} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497431 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366587 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d93c2e02-a22d-58cb-9c87-b91a9cb4dfcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366589} {:answer/id #uuid "10929a25-6dd4-5b6c-b2ff-f734fc2a5bc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366590} {:answer/id #uuid "72b528bb-8c58-5019-8054-8c8e0143306c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366591} {:answer/id #uuid "656c86e0-c53f-5510-a0dc-d24e1a0dfa5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366592} {:answer/id #uuid "67b89a6b-0190-5147-bf9d-6434bf3ec618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366593}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366589} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b327c5c-0866-5ee8-af76-fa3afa5af268" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "09857557-33a3-5971-b67f-bf80a841450b" :question/math-academy-id "q-95027" :question/problem "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=1-3{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=-3t+{t}^{2},\\,q(t)=2-3t-5{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\},$ and $[q(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=q(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(1-3{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(-3t+{t}^{2}) & =2-3t-5{t}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}t+(-3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}){t}^{2} & =2-3t-5{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=2 \\\\ -3{x}_{2} &=-3 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=-5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=2 \\\\ -3{x}_{2} &=-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations, we get ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nTherefore, $[q(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2+1=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "336b9f6a-688f-5c80-8b64-72381caefc8e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497489}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497463 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15ad0a48-81fc-5240-8a93-2ba2c02e3c92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357943} {:answer/id #uuid "298460b6-9c2f-56b8-8ced-e7d6ae2bf14d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358131} {:answer/id #uuid "506aad2b-31aa-5dd1-9a29-689248019180" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358442} {:answer/id #uuid "bdf66e93-e3a6-5ef3-b5de-a6b3e747467b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359407} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b3d49c-e9eb-52f4-b6f4-05e5551e3f23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359792}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359407} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9b78b52-cccd-55ae-b45f-93187dd919d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3294d22f-a9c0-5f7d-ab23-cc174247e4c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-95021" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{B}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $[A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & -3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} & 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 9{x}_{2} &=3 \\\\ -6{x}_{2} &=-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =1-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{2}{3}.$"} :step/id #uuid "a74c6d5d-0020-5707-b38c-10b2bc08bb70" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497431}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497470 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243954 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f04b25d-f38d-5fc1-9876-423c80368bba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242439} {:answer/id #uuid "b15931fb-52e5-5c41-a258-369e7d1fb1c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242724} {:answer/id #uuid "b42567a6-9890-5794-8a22-d6d56c1dc629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242754} {:answer/id #uuid "d0121691-eca2-5974-80e3-dbeade97f6d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243013} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e06cb0-4838-5459-9758-b2749164bf96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243380}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242724} :answer-field/id #uuid "e685e6a5-808d-5d60-91f6-26ddb332e292" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5dfc56d2-f4d4-58d9-9174-ebcc9a95025f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106781" :question/problem "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2+{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=1-t+2{t}^{2},\\,q(t)=6+2t.\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\},$ and $[q(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=q(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(2+{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(1-t+2{t}^{2}) & =6+2t \\\\ (2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})-{x}_{2}t+({x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}){t}^{2} & =6+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=6 \\\\ -{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=6 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} &=-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$  Substituting this into the first equation yields\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}+(-2)=6\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[q(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4+(-2)=2.$"} :step/id #uuid "bb742e8c-2853-5793-a821-ef78c316058a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497483 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497380 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04ec09a2-fbda-5a52-bb47-467878875739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496666} {:answer/id #uuid "44cab1fc-6315-55b0-b3f7-05e725aa34f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496806} {:answer/id #uuid "68265195-74f6-5911-84af-be7e3b4820a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496883} {:answer/id #uuid "c58ad0a2-710d-5e98-bc14-5b4a2dc8be59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497061} {:answer/id #uuid "d1293524-2d61-50b9-9952-84c3ff0dc264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497094}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497094} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fdc3716-f94a-5460-8503-fc942472a7e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8b164922-7a86-5e35-904e-baea511723b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-95012" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 9 & 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,{B}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -7 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $[A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 9 & 7\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -7 & 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} & 6{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} \\\\ 9{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & 7{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -7 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=4 \\\\ 9{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=-7 \\\\ 7{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2} &=9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 9 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 8 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 9 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 8 & 9\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 8 & 9\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+(-\\frac{7}{6}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{13}{2} & -13 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{13}{6} & \\frac{13}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=4 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{2}{x}_{2} &=-13 \\\\ \\frac{13}{6}{x}_{2} &=\\frac{13}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second, third and fourth equations, we get ${x}_{2}=2.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+5\\cdot 2 & =4 \\\\ 6{x}_{1} & =-6 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-1+2=1.$"} :step/id #uuid "eefaff6d-e472-53e9-a5c9-55ede9a20c93" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497463}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497489 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360542 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "180fb67f-6a67-5314-a5a7-83e4f4288271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357961} {:answer/id #uuid "5273d4ec-0ed6-56c2-ad25-8714176aa52c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358460} {:answer/id #uuid "947b00dd-5c3a-5d68-97b8-f3e2c0d73d6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359055} {:answer/id #uuid "a05b24ff-a2a2-55af-974a-b66d84b61f49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359151} {:answer/id #uuid "e8e73b2f-7a58-5798-826f-d0fc8ea33b00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359819}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359151} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8fa47fa-89cb-527e-bbec-2ad37148ba17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d482ef0a-c05c-5115-aa27-a3051b991631" :question/math-academy-id "q-95030" :question/problem "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2{t}^{2}+t-2,\\,{p}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}+3t-6,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=-2{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2},\\,\\,q(t)=-2{t}^{3}+4{t}^{2}+3t-6.\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\},$ and $[q(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},$ then ${x}_{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}{p}_{3}(t)=q(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(2{t}^{2}+t-2)+{x}_{2}({t}^{2}+3t-6)+{x}_{3}(-2{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}) & =-2{t}^{3}+4{t}^{2}+3t-6 \\\\ -2{x}_{3}{t}^{3}+(2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}){t}^{2}+({x}_{1}+3{x}_{2})t+(-2{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}) & =-2{t}^{3}+4{t}^{2}+3t-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{3} &=-2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=4 \\\\ {x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=3 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2} &=-6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -6 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -6 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{3} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -6 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=3 \\\\ -5{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=-2 \\\\ -2{x}_{3} &=-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=1.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-5{x}_{2}+3\\cdot 1=-2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the obtained values into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+3\\cdot 1=3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[q(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ and therefore ${x}_{2}=1.$"} :step/id #uuid "fe154e6d-10aa-5895-a8de-82e02aff4a99" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497470}}] :activity/title "The Coordinates of a Vector Relative to a Basis in Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496649}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565508} :activity/id #uuid "f79e0935-20bc-5760-b649-64062bd5ec18" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048333 :topic/id #uuid "95fa9a7c-5a38-5e0a-9686-d1451bc45075" :topic/math-academy-id 3212} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565504 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565421 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16f90a27-6313-5109-bbb8-4f7772524d5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564974} {:answer/id #uuid "2bd13daf-ca48-59e9-825a-a46dcf520048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565008} {:answer/id #uuid "9d32efd9-ca00-5319-9be6-643c6af3ff42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565179} {:answer/id #uuid "bd09020a-ccf6-5dd6-a0c1-117aa3597348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565226} {:answer/id #uuid "f157e119-b980-53fb-8f72-ef9064237542" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565302}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564974} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2e25b77-025f-5093-8bf8-7b6467b74964" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2e84799f-e1a6-5cc6-b626-825322837015" :question/math-academy-id "q-106779" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{B}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ 0 & {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=6 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=6 \\\\ {x}_{2}=4 \\\\ -{x}_{2}=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=4.$ From the first equation, we get  ${x}_{1}=6.$\n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=6+4=10.$"} :step/id #uuid "5373f673-5213-5338-a1d0-efbf6f780dfb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565530}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565508 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565478 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "107579c4-226c-56f4-a2e2-579b1beca778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564965} {:answer/id #uuid "722274c3-742b-5a48-b51d-ae59fbe68344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565111} {:answer/id #uuid "991ed807-57db-5621-8684-6e0503cebcf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565169} {:answer/id #uuid "e0e1b4b5-e50a-5c14-9473-88dec5bbe338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565271} {:answer/id #uuid "fb160850-a523-56ec-9e9b-f5b031875117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565322}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565322} :answer-field/id #uuid "358ca9bd-a430-5e36-accd-2c83f3de20f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "edf4a097-cde0-5ac1-86d1-cd429ba599d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-95018" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{B}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 9 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 9 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 6{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2} \\\\ -3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2} & 3{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=8 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=-5 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & -1 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{9}{2} & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=8 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -\\frac{9}{2}{x}_{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{2}{3}.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+9\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} & =8 \\\\ 6{x}_{1} & =2 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}=1.$"} :step/id #uuid "5f56d862-c86a-51b4-936f-ff4084ece379" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565518}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565518 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167330 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c89b52a5-e7a3-566a-9918-62a16e6087de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165190} {:answer/id #uuid "f55fc263-98d6-5acb-9fd4-97e311d62182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165191} {:answer/id #uuid "02da13f5-fbac-5188-9cb2-808df4049ce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165192} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce84c4e-ac4e-5985-bea6-5f1e1ad5ff0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165193} {:answer/id #uuid "30554f8d-ed15-55d9-9885-c078507c90c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165194}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186165194} :answer-field/id #uuid "1590187d-85cc-5ce7-9d74-f442d233c0fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "431d0042-1670-53b6-9a71-1178cea2315a" :question/math-academy-id "q-95022" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{B}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1} & 7{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ -5{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} & 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}=-2 \\\\ 7{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{7}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}=-2 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ -{x}_{2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=2.$ From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-1.$ \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-1+2=1.$"} :step/id #uuid "8e5bef8a-6e29-5055-9cfa-a6cc36b1ecde" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565527}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565527 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565420 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77f754b6-3412-584d-ba89-67035e23606c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565119} {:answer/id #uuid "9749750d-293f-5863-817e-e6019efcfb73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565166} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d21a6a-0db9-5004-bce9-f0f22743dbc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565184} {:answer/id #uuid "ba52f496-ec86-590f-89ec-2e6d908c3e02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565220} {:answer/id #uuid "e63f7738-750c-5af7-b550-1409a521290c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565280}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565119} :answer-field/id #uuid "eef364c2-b743-5663-ae5b-bf40b13e0fd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2d45985d-1bef-55fa-a31c-73d51b4f78be" :question/math-academy-id "q-95029" :question/problem "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=-1-t+2{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t-{t}^{2},\\,q(t)=3-3t.\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\},$ and $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=q(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(-1-t+2{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(t-{t}^{2}) & =3-3t \\\\ -{x}_{1}+(-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})t+(2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}){t}^{2} & =3-3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ {x}_{2}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations, we get ${x}_{1}=-3$ and ${x}_{2}=-6.$ \n\nTherefore, $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-3+(-6)=-9.$"} :step/id #uuid "bcb7938f-e53a-5c04-9c08-1c79bbb07158" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565504}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565530 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4beb3a66-909a-5284-8f19-4108f422c07d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565048} {:answer/id #uuid "b8494486-b6be-5318-969d-dd99f4d62997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565215} {:answer/id #uuid "c322b0fe-2579-53a9-a017-e308cf16a5b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565234} {:answer/id #uuid "d7634f67-a8f6-59d4-a750-b899dde278d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565257} {:answer/id #uuid "e34854be-3584-5770-a376-eca2a97d5e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565277}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565277} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae5852dc-829f-545a-9915-7bda8a3606ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "881394e4-d1e2-502e-8f1c-cafafb97686c" :question/math-academy-id "q-106782" :question/problem "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2-t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=3-2{t}^{2},\\,q(t)=3t-4{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\},$ and $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=q(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(2-t)+{x}_{2}(3-2{t}^{2}) & =3t-4{t}^{2} \\\\ (2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2})-{x}_{1}t-2{x}_{2}{t}^{2} & =3t-4{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations we get ${x}_{2}=2.$ From the  first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-3+2=-1.$"} :step/id #uuid "c426b9e2-f2e5-5535-8167-63c608a5c5aa"}] :activity/title "The Coordinates of a Vector Relative to a Basis in Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564937}]}
